\f0\fs24 \cf0 Note that the firewall daemon, firewalld, on Manjaro, blocks multicast; something on OpenBSD is interfering with mDNS but not multicast, since logging works.\
\
Rough logic flow, non-Registry service or utility:\
1. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
2. Wait for the number of seconds of a retry interval.\
3. If there is no response within the retry interval and there are retry intervals left, go back to the first step.\
4. If there is a response, 
\f1\b record
\f0\b0  the connection details.\
5. Close the Registry multicast port.\
6. If there was no response, perform the mDNS search for a Registry.\
7. Exit if there was no response.\
8. Proceed with normal activity.\
\
Rough logic flow, Registry service:\
1. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
2. Wait for the number of seconds of a retry interval.\
3. If there is no response within the retry interval and there are retry intervals left, go back to the first step.\
4. If there was no response, perform the mDNS search for a Registry.\
5. If there is a response, 
\f1\b report
\f0\b0  the connection details and exit.\
6. Start the Registry TCP/IP request listener.\
7. Start the mDNS responder thread.\
8. If a request arrives on the Registry multicast port, respond with the details of the Registry TCP/IP request listener.\
9. If a request arrives on the Registry TCP/IP request port, process it and go back to the previous step unless the request is to stop.\
\
Note that this is similarly structured to the mDNS code, but is simpler since very little needs to be transferred.\
The multicast send and receive code already exists as part of nImOlog and nImOpost,\
\
Note that SearchContext is the parent of ServiceContext and UtilityContext and is widely referenced; the initial code should be parallel to the mDNS support.\
